# Generated from: specs/16-define-volunteer-privacy-retention-and-deletion-lifecycle/requirements.md
Feature: Define volunteer privacy retention and deletion lifecycle
  As an accountless volunteer
  I want to understand and control the contact data used for scheduling
  So that participation does not create indefinite or misleading personal records

  @SCN001
  Scenario: Contact collection is informed and minimized
    Given a volunteer opens a service-commitment request form
    When the form displays name, email, and optional phone fields
    Then privacy text before submit explains purpose, coordinator visibility, one-year retention, backup rotation, and the configured removal contact
    And the text says the record is for service scheduling, not AA membership or attendance
    When the volunteer submits without a phone
    Then the request succeeds and phone remains null

  @SCN002
  Scenario: Expired eligible contact is anonymized automatically
    Given a volunteer has no pending request, active assignment, or future commitment
    And the greatest related workflow activity or shift end is more than 365 days old
    And usable request-status and assignment-action links and notification destinations remain
    When the retention process locks and revalidates the volunteer
    Then name and email become neutral non-personal tombstones
    And phone is cleared
    And all private links are unusable
    And notification destinations are redacted
    And one non-identifying VolunteerAnonymized audit commits atomically
    And non-identifying workflow history remains valid

  @SCN003
  Scenario Outline: Live workflow state blocks removal
    Given a volunteer has a <dependency>
    When automatic retention or a coordinator attempts removal
    Then the operation explains the blocking category and count
    And no contact, token, destination, workflow, or audit state changes

    Examples:
      | dependency          |
      | pending request     |
      | assigned assignment |
      | confirmed assignment|
      | future commitment   |

  @SCN004
  Scenario: Coordinator processes a verified early-removal request
    Given a volunteer supplies their email and one recent commitment out of band
    And an allowlisted coordinator matches both values
    And the volunteer has no live dependency
    When the coordinator confirms removal before 365 days
    Then eligible contact is anonymized through the same atomic command
    And the success text says non-identifying scheduling history was retained
    And the audit names the coordinator and reason without removed values

  @SCN005
  Scenario: Contact reuse cannot race anonymization
    Given one PostgreSQL transaction is creating a request or assignment for an existing volunteer
    And another transaction attempts to anonymize that volunteer
    When both operations acquire the shared volunteer-row lock and commit
    Then one serially valid state results
    And an anonymized record never regains contact data or a live dependency
    And a later request using the removed email creates a new volunteer record

  @SCN006
  Scenario: Concurrent retention is idempotent
    Given two retention workers select the same eligible volunteer
    When both process the candidate
    Then the volunteer is anonymized once
    And exactly one VolunteerAnonymized audit exists
    And both workers can continue processing later bounded candidates
